From fdea15709b8bd4133796b08ec6a0a3376f85371b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Florent Merlet <florentmerlet73@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 29 Oct 2026 11:14:10 +0100
Subject: [PATCH 6/7] TIP 759 (6/7): raw commands in expressions (provisional)

In an expression, a command without brackets is an operand when its name
is one of: concat dict for foreach global if incr lappend lassign lindex
llength lmap lrange lset namespace return string switch upvar variable
while, followed by a space. It ends at ";", or at an operator or a close
paren that begins a word: llength $L + 1 is [llength $L] + 1. This makes
expression scripts such as {( s = 0; foreach u $U v $V {( s = $s+$u*$v )};
$s )} possible. Provisional: to be reviewed with a register of the
command names.

- tclCharTypeTable: TYPE_OP for the operator characters (the table and
  the type variables become int; the TYPE_NORMAL fast paths ignore it).
- Tcl_ParseCommand: nested == TCL_CMD_NESTED_IN_EXPR ends a command at
  TYPE_OP and TYPE_CLOSE_PAREN too.
- ParseLexeme: COMMAND lexeme; ParseExpr: TCL_TOKEN_CMD_IN_EXPR token
  (new token type, 512); TclCompileTokens compiles it as a script.
- testexprparser shows it as "rawcommand".
- Tests: exprRawCmd.test. Docs: expr.n.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01MrNWAEyXLqekovNBvbMZ4z
